# Triage Factory — multi-mode environment template.
# Copy to .env, fill in the values below, then:
#   ./triagefactory jwk-init --write-env .env   # populates GOTRUE_JWT_KEYS, GOTRUE_JWT_SECRET, TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN
#   docker compose up -d
#
# Blank required values use ${VAR:?} in docker-compose.yml, so an unfilled
# .env fails fast at `docker compose up` rather than booting on placeholders.
# Commented lines (# VAR=default) are optional overrides showing their default.
#
# Keys/passwords marked `openssl rand -hex 32` want 32 random bytes as HEX, not
# base64: compose interpolates some directly into URL-form DSNs where base64's
# `/ + =` are reserved and break the parser; hex sidesteps that and keeps every
# credential uniform for rotation runbooks.
#
# File-backed secrets: a secret can be supplied from a file instead — set
# <NAME>_FILE to a mount path (e.g. TF_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/
# enc_key, leaving the plain <NAME> blank). The binary reads it at boot, unsets
# it from the env (so children don't inherit it), and — because the value was
# never an env var — keeps it out of `docker inspect` / /proc/environ too. File
# wins if both are set; an unreadable path fails boot. The bundled compose
# forwards <NAME>_FILE for the TF-only secrets (TF_LICENSE,
# TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN, TF_SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY, TF_COOKIE_SECRET,
# TF_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY, and — on control only — TF_ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_SECRET
# plus the three deployment GitHub App secrets); the DB
# DSN + blob/authenticator secrets are constructed or shared with a sidecar, so
# _FILE applies to them only in a custom (non-bundled-compose) deployment. See
# docs/self-hosting/secrets.md.

# ── Postgres role passwords ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Four roles with separate passwords so a compromise of one pool doesn't
# surrender the others. The postgres-postinit sidecars re-apply them on every
# `docker compose up` (rotate here, then re-up). All hex (see note above).
#
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD             superuser — migrations + admin; NOT the app-
#                                 pool password. Interpolated into a URL-form
#                                 DSN, so hex is required, not just preferred.
#   SUPABASE_AUTH_ADMIN_PASSWORD  GoTrue's DB role (supabase_auth_admin). Also
#                                 URL-form; hex required.
#   TF_AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD     the `authenticator` role TF's app pool uses
#                                 for RLS-active request handling.
#   TF_SYSTEM_PASSWORD            least-privilege role executor pods' admin pool
#                                 uses instead of supabase_admin (BYPASSRLS but
#                                 no DDL + a narrow grant list; see the tf_system
#                                 section of the pg baseline migration). Ships
#                                 NOLOGIN; the postgres-postinit-system sidecar
#                                 sets LOGIN + password after migrations run
#                                 (tf_system doesn't exist until then).
#                                 control/all pods keep the supabase_admin DSN.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
SUPABASE_AUTH_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
TF_AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD=
TF_SYSTEM_PASSWORD=

# Public-facing URL of this deployment (OAuth redirect URIs, GoTrue issuer
# claim, cookie domain). No trailing slash.
TF_PUBLIC_URL=

# Disable self-service org creation (invite-only). Default false leaves the
# "create your organization" affordance on (right for SaaS / open self-hosts);
# true leaves only the invite path. Boolean, case/space-tolerant. Local ignores.
# TF_PREVENT_ORG_CREATION=false

# ── Deployment role + scaling ──────────────────────────────────────────
# TF_ROLE splits the process across cooperating pods:
#   control   API/WS + migrations + the background brain (pollers, router, AI
#             managers). Never claims runs; no sandbox caps.
#   executor  shared-nothing sandbox worker: run dispatcher + sandboxes + per-run
#             credential sidecars + reapers. No user HTTP, no pollers, no
#             migrations (asserts the schema at boot).
# Multi mode is ALWAYS this split: TF_ROLE is required, and unset / the retired
# "all" / a typo fails boot loudly. The bundled docker-compose.yml sets the role
# per service — set it yourself only in a custom (non-compose) deployment. Local
# mode ignores TF_ROLE (single-process, gated on TF_MODE). Scale out with
# `docker compose up -d --scale executor=N`; see docs/self-hosting/scaling.md.

# Executor localhost liveness port (executor only), the HEALTHCHECK target at
# 127.0.0.1:<port>/healthz. Default 3001. No auth (loopback, no tenant data).
# TF_HEALTHZ_PORT=3001

# Cross-pod run control (TFAC-585): how long a control pod waits for the owning
# executor to ack an interrupt/steer before giving up (504). Cancel is always
# fire-and-forget; permission answers use a fixed 10s. Default 5s; invalid →
# default + boot warning. Multi only (local always owns its own run).
# TF_SIGNAL_ACK_TIMEOUT=5s
# How long an acked conversation_signals row is kept before the purge reaper deletes it
# (audit convenience, not correctness — unacked rows expire harmlessly). Multi.
# TF_CONVERSATION_SIGNAL_PURGE_AFTER=24h

# ── Background-brain leader lease (TFAC-583, control only) ────────────────
# One control pod at a time runs the brain (pollers, router, AI managers,
# sweepers); standbys wait to take over. Positive integer seconds. DEMOTE must
# be < TTL (boot refuses otherwise) so a demoted holder provably stops before a
# successor acquires. Defaults target ~30s worst-case failover — widen on a
# high-latency control↔Postgres link, tighten (keeping the margin) for faster
# failover. Ignored at all/local (single process self-holds, zero lease I/O).
# TF_LEASE_RENEW_SEC=5
# TF_LEASE_TTL_SEC=20
# TF_LEASE_DEMOTE_SEC=15

# ── Fleet reaper + partition self-fence (TFAC-586, control/all) ───────────
# The leader requeues (or terminal-fails with failure_kind='executor_lost' past
# the attempt budget) runs whose executor's heartbeat went stale, and tombstones
# instances rows abandoned 7+ days. TF_SELF_FENCE_SEC is the executor-side
# partner: how long an instance tolerates its own heartbeat WRITE failing before
# it kills its sandboxes and stops claiming (reversible; distinct from the
# sticky identity-supersession fence). SELF_FENCE < REAPER_STALE is a correctness
# property (boot refuses otherwise): a partitioned executor must provably stop
# before the reaper requeues its rows. Defaults heartbeat 4s < self-fence 15s <
# reaper 30s carry that margin; widen together (keep the gap) on a slow link.
# TF_SELF_FENCE_SEC=15
# TF_REAPER_STALE_SEC=30
# Consecutive losses (across executors, via reaper requeue) before a
# dead-executor loss terminal-fails instead of requeuing. Counted per episode,
# not per lifetime: any engagement that recorded an outcome of its own — it
# parked, it was stopped, it concluded — starts the count over, so only a real
# crash loop exhausts it. Retried external writes are absorbed by upsert
# semantics. Default 2.
# TF_MAX_CLAIM_ATTEMPTS=2

# How long a parked workspace TREE sits idle on an executor's disk before the
# hourly sweep reclaims it (the durable snapshot blob has its own, much longer,
# 14-day retention — this bounds the warm copy). A tree is evicted only once its
# snapshot is verifiably written, nothing shares it under a live claim, and the
# whole key is past this window; a resume after that rehydrates from the blob.
# Non-negative integer seconds, 0 disables. Default 6h in multi; local mode
# defaults to disabled and opts in by setting this.
# TF_WORKSPACE_EVICT_AFTER_SEC=21600

# Placement affinity (TFAC-587): a capacity-weighted rendezvous hash keeps a
# hot repo's clone/worktree cache warm on its owner. Advisory — the queue is the
# truth, so any executor can run any run and a saturated/dead owner's work spills
# to spare capacity. ON in multi, forced off in local (N=1). off drops the layer
# (claim reverts to global-oldest).
# TF_PLACEMENT=on
# Tier-2 aging: how long a queued run waits for its owner before any executor may
# claim it (a dead/gated/draining owner spills immediately regardless). ~15-30s.
# TF_PLACEMENT_AGING_SEC=20
# Heartbeat staleness past which a stamped owner is treated as dead, so its runs
# spill without waiting out the aging window.
# TF_PLACEMENT_LIVENESS_SEC=12

# Postgres pool ceiling, per pool (multi opens an admin + an app pool). Empty →
# per-role default: 25 control/all, 6 executor (idle app pool). Floored at 2.
# Raise alongside Postgres max_connections when scaling — see scaling.md.
# TF_DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=
# Advisory platform-memory reserve (MiB) for the boot capacity log only. Empty →
# 12288 control/all (co-resident PG/GoTrue/blob) or 2048 executor. Runtime
# protection is TF_MAX_CONCURRENT_CLAIMS + the dispatch floor, not this.
# TF_PLATFORM_RESERVE_MB=

# ── WS backplane (TFAC-584, multi only) ──────────────────────────────────
# Cross-pod fan-out for the websocket Hub, session kick, and brain-bound run
# sentinels (spec §5.1/§5.3). A single all-in-one process never touches it. All
# optional; unset = the documented default.
#
# Session-mode (non-pooled) DSN for the fabric's dedicated LISTEN connections
# (tf_ctl, and tf_wake on dispatcher roles). Empty → TF_DATABASE_URL (direct
# today). Set separately once a transaction-mode pooler (TFAC-307) fronts
# TF_DATABASE_URL — LISTEN can't ride a pooled connection.
# TF_DATABASE_DIRECT_URL=
# Largest tf_ws payload that rides inline on the NOTIFY before falling back to a
# ws_outbox row. Default 6144B; clamped at Postgres's ~8000B NOTIFY ceiling.
# TF_WS_INLINE_MAX_B=6144
# ws_outbox row TTL (s): a reader that misses a row drops the event (lossy by
# contract). Default 60.
# TF_WS_OUTBOX_TTL_SECONDS=60
# How often a control pod upserts its sockets' presence into ws_presence
# (cross-pod fast-deny). Default 15.
# TF_WS_PRESENCE_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=15
# How long a presence row reads as live after its last heartbeat — a READ-time
# filter, deletes nothing. Keep comfortably above the heartbeat so one missed
# beat doesn't flip to "absent". Default 45.
# TF_WS_PRESENCE_TTL_SECONDS=45
# How long a presence row survives before the reaper deletes it — storage bound
# only (every reconnect mints a new row nothing overwrites). Much larger than the
# TTL above. Default 300.
# TF_WS_PRESENCE_ROW_TTL_SECONDS=300
# Session-kick backstop: each control pod re-validates its sockets' sessions
# against the DB on this cadence, bounding a lost tf_ctl kick at one interval
# (the NOTIFY is the fast path; this is the guarantee). Default 60.
# TF_WS_SESSION_REVALIDATE_SECONDS=60

# ── Claim concurrency + memory ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Concurrent claims on this host — delegated conversations.
# Default 8 (concurrency is also an API-spend throttle). Capacity is
# memory-bound: ~0.25 GB RAM per claim (engine pages are shared) plus a
# platform reserve —
#   claims ≈ (RAM − 12 GB) ÷ 0.25 GB     e.g. 64 GB host → ~200 claims
# (docs/benchmarks/sandbox-bench.md). Boot logs derived capacity and warns if the
# cap exceeds it. >256 clamps to 256 (each claim owns a /24 out of one /16).
# Invalid → default + warning.
# TF_MAX_CONCURRENT_CLAIMS=8
# Dispatch memory guardrail (MiB): the dispatcher stops claiming when host
# MemAvailable drops below this (conversations stay queued, resume when memory
# recovers) — stops a too-high cap from dispatching into the OOM killer.
# Default 4096; 0 disables; fails open where memory isn't reportable
# (non-Linux). Invalid → default + warning.
# TF_DISPATCH_MEM_FLOOR_MB=4096
# Per-run memory ceiling (MiB): each sandbox's cgroup gets memory.max = this
# (swap 0), so one runaway run OOM-kills alone with a clear error. Default 4096
# (~16x the planning budget — real builds spike; this is host protection, not
# budget). 0 disables. Needs `cgroup: private` (see docker-compose.yml); fails
# open with one warning where cgroup delegation is unavailable.
# TF_CLAIM_MEMORY_LIMIT_MB=4096
# Per-run cap on concurrent in-flight Jira/GitHub REST calls one run's agent may
# make (each upstream counted separately). A per-run governor on how hard a
# single agent hits the org bot identity that every run shares; normal
# parallelism stays well under the default, so this bounds a pathological burst
# rather than throttling ordinary work. The fleet-wide protection against the
# shared per-account rate limit is the clients' 429/Retry-After backoff, not
# this. Default 8; <=0 disables the cap. Invalid → default + warning.
# TF_AGENTHOST_MAX_UPSTREAM_CONCURRENCY=8
# How long (seconds) a resume waits for a workspace snapshot whose durable
# state says a persist is still in flight, before falling back to a fresh
# workspace. A stop parks the conversation before the blob is written, so this
# is a resume on another executor catching up with the one still writing. It
# ends as soon as the blob lands, the persist records a failure, or the writing
# executor stops heartbeating — a hung-writer backstop, not an expected wait.
# Default 60; must be positive (a zero wait would discard work a writer was
# about to store). Invalid → default + warning.
# TF_SNAPSHOT_WAIT_SEC=60

# ── Privilege separation (multi + Linux) ─────────────────────────────────
# The sandbox's netns/iptables/cgroup/rootfs setup + run launch route through a
# separate cap-broker process (the only sandbox launch path, spawned
# unconditionally); the orchestrator drops all capabilities at exec
# (docker/entrypoint.sh) and only the cap-broker holds CAP_SYS_ADMIN/NET_ADMIN.
# Local never sandboxes. No flag disables it. See docs/security/privilege-separation.md.
#
# Advanced (match the image's baked-in tf-orchestrator user + /run layout;
# override only for a custom base image). The orchestrator's HOME must follow the
# real user so the Claude Code SDK session state resolves. TF_SANDBOX_GID is the
# one supplementary group carried through the drop, so per-run agenthost sockets
# can be granted to the sandbox identity without CAP_CHOWN.
# TF_ORCHESTRATOR_UID=10001
# TF_ORCHESTRATOR_GID=10001
# TF_ORCHESTRATOR_HOME=/home/tf-orchestrator
# TF_CAPBROKER_SOCKET=/run/tf/cap-broker.sock
# TF_SANDBOX_GID=10000

# ── Bedrock role mode: short-lived LLM credentials (TFAC-616, multi) ──────
# When an org's Bedrock auth is `role`, it stores only a customer IAM Role ARN +
# a TF-generated External ID (no secret). The control process assumes that role
# as its OWN ambient AWS identity (an instance role, or AWS_* on the control
# service here — a deployment secret, not a per-user credential) and mints
# short-lived STS creds scoped to bedrock:InvokeModel* on the org's model,
# attributed per run in the customer's CloudTrail. Bearer / access-key / Anthropic
# auth are unchanged passthrough. See docs/self-host-setup.md.
#
# STS mint TTL (s). Default 3600 (the one-hour cap); floored at 900.
# TF_LLM_CRED_TTL_SEC=3600
# Executor egress binding (both default unset): add a network condition to an
# executor-bound mint so a lifted credential is unusable off its egress —
# EGRESS_CIDRS → aws:SourceIp, VPCE_IDS → aws:SourceVpce (comma-separated).
# Brain-bound mints (scorer, profiler, connect probe) never carry one.
# TF_EXECUTOR_EGRESS_CIDRS=10.0.0.0/8
# TF_EXECUTOR_VPCE_IDS=vpce-0abc123,vpce-0def456

# ── Client-IP / trusted proxy (multi) ────────────────────────────────────
# clientIP feeds session forensics, the SOC2 auth audit log, and the pre-auth
# per-IP rate limiter. X-Forwarded-For is client-appended and spoofable, so it's
# honored ONLY when the direct peer is a proxy listed here (clientIP then walks
# XFF right→left to the first untrusted hop).
#   directly exposed → leave unset (client IP = direct peer, unspoofable).
#   behind an LB/CDN → set the proxy egress CIDR(s) AND strip inbound XFF at the
#     edge; otherwise every request collapses onto the LB's IP (one global rate
#     bucket, audit log records the LB). Comma-separated CIDRs (bare IP = /32|/128);
#     literal "none" == TF_CAPTURE_CLIENT_IP=false.
# TF_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR=10.0.0.0/8,192.168.0.0/16
# Capture client IPs at all. Default true; false (data minimization) stores NULL
# and degrades the per-IP limiter to one global bucket. Boolean.
# TF_CAPTURE_CLIENT_IP=true

# ── Traces ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OTLP/HTTP endpoint each pod pushes finished spans to. Defaults to the
# bundled `tempo` service, so traces work out of the box; Grafana reads them
# at http://localhost:3030 (loopback only).
#
# Set this to use a different backend — Tempo, Jaeger, an OTel Collector, a
# vendor endpoint — or to `off` to disable tracing, which is also required if
# you delete the tempo service from docker-compose.yml. Bare host:port is read
# as http://. Standard OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_* knobs (headers, TLS, compression)
# reach the exporter, but the two OTEL endpoint vars never enable tracing on
# their own.
#
# Spans carry opaque IDs (org.id, conversation.id, task.id) and never repo
# names, usernames, PR titles, or message text — but they are unauthenticated
# on the wire unless the backend does TLS, so keep the collector inside the
# compose network / cluster. See docs/self-hosting/monitoring.md.
# TF_TRACES_ENDPOINT=http://tempo:4318

# ── GitHub OAuth app ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Create at https://github.com/settings/developers with callback
# ${TF_PUBLIC_URL}/auth/v1/callback.
GH_CLIENT_ID=
GH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── GoTrue JWT signing (jwk-init writes these) ───────────────────────────
# `triagefactory jwk-init --write-env .env` writes GOTRUE_JWT_KEYS,
# GOTRUE_JWT_SECRET, and TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN (secret material — keep
# this file off VCS). The first two are read by gotrue; the service-role token by
# the TF binary (SSO, below). jwk-init upserts each in place, so re-running is
# clean and REUSES an existing keypair (only --rotate rolls it, forcing re-auth).

# ── SAML SSO (Microsoft Entra et al.), multi ─────────────────────────────
# SSO runs through GoTrue as a SAML SP. Connections are configured PER-ORG at
# runtime (an admin pastes their IdP metadata URL into the in-app "Configure SSO"
# surface) — no env-driven provider. OFF by default; a fresh `docker compose up`
# boots on GitHub-OAuth login alone. Full walkthrough: docs/sso-entra.md.
#
# To enable, do BOTH: uncomment GOTRUE_SAML_ENABLED=true (GoTrue's feature switch;
# it does NOT infer it from the key — without it the SAML routes TF registers
# providers through aren't mounted), and set GOTRUE_SAML_PRIVATE_KEY.
# GOTRUE_SAML_ENABLED=true
#
# GOTRUE_SAML_PRIVATE_KEY signs GoTrue's outbound AuthnRequests — an RSA key (NOT
# ES256) in DER, base64-encoded; a DIFFERENT key from the session-JWT material.
# Required only when SAML is enabled (enabled + blank → GoTrue won't boot).
# Generate ONCE and keep stable (rotating invalidates every registered provider):
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm rsa -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -outform DER | base64 -w0
GOTRUE_SAML_PRIVATE_KEY=

# The RS256 service-role JWT the TF binary presents to GoTrue's admin SSO API
# when an org registers an IdP. jwk-init mints + writes it (same key as
# GOTRUE_JWT_KEYS) — don't set by hand; TF holds only this token, never the key.
# Optional (SSO only); set TF_PUBLIC_URL before jwk-init so its iss matches.
# TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN=

# ── At-rest encryption keys (32 random bytes each) ───────────────────────
# All three are AES-GCM / HMAC keys; generate with `openssl rand -hex 32` (base64
# also accepted). Kept distinct — never derived from one master — so they rotate
# independently. Rotation impact differs, and is the reason to keep them apart:
#   TF_SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY  encrypts access/refresh tokens at rest in
#                              public.sessions. Rotating invalidates every
#                              session → plan a forced re-auth.
#   TF_COOKIE_SECRET           HMAC for the short-lived OAuth state cookie (PKCE
#                              verifier + CSRF). Rotating only breaks in-flight
#                              handshakes (10-min window) — active sessions fine.
#   TF_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY   encrypts org + per-user integration secrets
#                              (GitHub App PEM/client-secret/webhook-secret, PATs,
#                              Jira tokens) at rest in public.org_secrets — app-
#                              side ciphertext, not Supabase Vault, so a Postgres
#                              recreate can't lose them (TFAC-402). Rotating
#                              invalidates every stored secret → operators re-enter.
TF_SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
TF_COOKIE_SECRET=
TF_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── Durable blob storage (object store), multi ───────────────────────────
# The blueprint workspace is snapshotted to an S3-compatible store so it outlives
# its executor. Any S3 backend works (SeaweedFS, S3, R2, Supabase Storage).
# The binary REFUSES TO BOOT without TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT + TF_BLOB_BUCKET in multi;
# local ignores all of this and writes under ~/.triagefactory/blobs.
#
# Self-host runs the bundled `seaweedfs`: fill in the two creds below (the stack
# templates them into SeaweedFS's identity AND TF's client, creates the bucket,
# and points ENDPOINT at http://seaweedfs:8333). ENDPOINT/BUCKET/REGION default
# for self-host — leave them unset. Generate each with `openssl rand -hex 32`
# (access key ≥3 chars, secret ≥8 — 64-hex satisfies both and stays JSON/URL-safe).
TF_BLOB_ACCESS_KEY=
TF_BLOB_SECRET_KEY=
#
# SaaS / BYO: set the creds to your backend's keys and uncomment the overrides.
# Same client, different endpoint; the seaweedfs service still starts but goes
# unused (if you remove it in an override, also drop seaweedfs-postinit from
# triagefactory's depends_on).
#   TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT  scheme://host[:port][/base/path] — scheme selects TLS, base
#                     path preserved (S3/R2 https://host, or Supabase
#                     https://<ref>.supabase.co/storage/v1/s3). Default http://seaweedfs:8333
#   TF_BLOB_BUCKET    workspace-blob bucket; pre-create it. Default tf-workspaces
#   TF_BLOB_REGION    S3 region (S3/Supabase want it, SeaweedFS ignores). Default us-east-1
# TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT=
# TF_BLOB_BUCKET=
# TF_BLOB_REGION=

# ── Enterprise license (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Signed license token from your vendor; unlocks Enterprise features (SSO, Slack,
# governance, fleet console). Blank = all EE off. Logs
# `Enterprise license: <org> (features: [...])` on success.
TF_LICENSE=
# Dev/fork issuers only: override the baked verification key at image-build time
# (compose passes this to the TF build as a build arg). Leave unset to verify
# official licenses. Use of this to circumvent license key
# functionality in production violates the license
# TF_LICENSE_PUBLIC_KEY=

# ── Atlassian OAuth app (first-party, optional) ──────────────────────────
# The first-party 3LO app for one-click "Connect Jira" on Cloud orgs. Deployment
# config (read here in multi, not the keychain) because it's one shared app for
# the whole deployment; local mode ignores these and users supply a BYO app from
# Settings. Unset → no hosted app (each org can still add its own, which wins).
# Register at https://developer.atlassian.com/console/myapps/ with callback
# ${TF_PUBLIC_URL}/api/orgs/{org}/jira/connect/callback.
# TF_ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_ID=
# TF_ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Deployment GitHub App (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────
# One GitHub App registered by hand for the whole deployment, so a workspace
# admin can press "Connect GitHub" and pick an account instead of registering
# an App of their own. Deployment config (read here in multi, never the
# keychain); local mode ignores these. Set all four or none: a strict subset is
# logged at boot and treated as none. Unset changes nothing — every workspace
# keeps bringing its own App or PAT — and a workspace that has its own App
# keeps using it either way; the deployment App is only what a workspace with
# no credential gets. Registration walkthrough (permissions, the OAuth-during-
# install setting, both URLs): docs/self-hosting/install.md, "Register a
# deployment GitHub App". The App ID is the numeric id, not the client id; the
# slug and client id are read back from GitHub. The private key is a multi-line
# PEM, which a .env carries badly — supply it as a mounted file via
# TF_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE (see docs/self-hosting/secrets.md).
# TF_GITHUB_APP_ID=
# TF_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# TF_GITHUB_APP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# TF_GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The deployment's default GitHub: the web base every workspace with no GitHub
# base URL of its own resolves to, and the GitHub the deployment App above is
# registered on — one variable because they are one fact. Unset means
# https://github.com. A deployment on GitHub Enterprise Server sets it BEFORE
# the first workspace exists: installation, reachability and identity rows are
# keyed under the host they were written with and nothing migrates them, so
# changing it later is a fresh-install decision. Plain config, not a secret;
# forwarded to both pods. A value that is not an http(s) web base fails boot.
# TF_DEFAULT_GITHUB_HOST=https://github.com
